# Versioned MechanismRecord objects.
{"assumptions":["Hash collisions are tolerable at the chosen table size / vocab.","The learned gate can down-weight noisy n-gram rows.","Benefit persists in the 2000-step repeated-data regime, not only fresh-stream training."],"causal_chain":["Local bigram/trigram context carries next-token information a token-only embedding misses.","Hashed n-gram embedding tables cache this signal and, gated into the attention value path, add it cheaply per token.","Sparse n-gram rows are updated rarely, so they benefit from a higher effective learning rate than dense matrices.","The extra local signal reduces per-token loss, most where token-only context is weakest."],"claim_ids":["clm_modded_nanogpt_1","clm_modded_nanogpt_3","clm_r2_ngram_n_grammer_1","clm_r2_ngram_n_grammer_2","clm_r2_ngram_scone_scaling_embeddings_1","clm_r2_ngram_scone_scaling_embeddings_3","clm_r2_ngram_infini_gram_3"],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"The baseline already injects per-token value embeddings into the attention value path; extending this with hashed bigram/trigram embedding tables gated into the value path gives the model a cheap local n-gram (short-context) memory, lowering loss without the cost of convolutional/attention-heavy alternatives. In the repeated-data regime this is a high-value, under-explored axis (Recursive report it as one of their biggest gains).","fingerprint":"88fc5d5607e8bed2","intervention_predictions":[{"expected_change":"vs this control, an enriched hashed n-gram value-embedding (larger tables / added gated trigram path / retuned sparse LR) lowers 2000-step val_bpb beyond the 0.0007 floor","intervention_id":"int_current_schedule"}],"mechanism_id":"mech_ngram_short_context_memory","name":"Hashed n-gram value-embedding short-context memory","notes":"Seeded 2026-07-21 as the anchor exploration direction (Recursive 'short-context memory' result). Preserve; do not archive.","observable_predictions":[{"expected_pattern":"lower at matched step vs baseline when n-gram memory is enriched","observable_id":"obs_raw_train_loss"},{"expected_pattern":"watch for widening (memorization) if n-gram capacity is raised too far in the repeated-data regime","observable_id":"obs_train_val_gap"}],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"literature","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT (rsi/daniel)","regime":"repeated-data (~1.6 epoch)","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"active","tags":["ngram","value_embedding","short_context_memory","architecture","explore","priority"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["Stays underfit.","No late instability."],"causal_chain":["Underfit at 2000 steps.","Baseline warmdown starts at 5%.","Lower ratio = longer high-LR phase.","Small lower final val_bpb."],"claim_ids":["clm_wsd_schedule_1","clm_wsd_schedule_2","clm_sched_lr_wsd_constant_cooldown_1","clm_sched_lr_wsd_constant_cooldown_2"],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"Underfit at 2000 steps; longer full-LR stable phase = more high-LR descent; WSD cooldown still captures the drop -> lower final val_bpb.","fingerprint":"f443d00fe2c79c0a","intervention_predictions":[{"expected_change":"small negative delta; near/below seed floor","intervention_id":"int_warmdown_075"}],"mechanism_id":"mech_lr_stable_phase_convergence","name":"Longer full-LR stable phase speeds underfit convergence","notes":"Formalized 2026-07-22 through the gate.","observable_predictions":[{"expected_pattern":"lower final val_loss at longer stable phase","observable_id":"obs_val_loss"}],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"literature","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel","regime":"underfit","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"active","tags":["schedule","wsd","convergence"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["SHUFFLE_DATA preserves content.","The regime is constrained enough for order to matter.","Decorrelation helps more than any lost sequential structure."],"causal_chain":["Baseline strictly-sequential -> correlated minibatches + fixed repeated-data order.","Reshuffle-between-epochs decorrelates visitation (seeded, deterministic, content-preserving).","In the constrained/repeated regime this reduces order-overfitting / improves optimization.","Lower per-token loss -> lower val_bpb."],"claim_ids":["clm_data_when_do_curricula_work_1","clm_data_when_do_curricula_work_2","clm_data_curriculum_learning_1"],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"Baseline presents the corpus strictly-sequentially (correlated minibatches; fixed repeated-data order). Literature: data-presentation ORDER affects the outcome mainly in CONSTRAINED regimes (limited compute/data) -- our setting. A reshuffle-between-epochs policy (SHUFFLE_DATA) decorrelates the stream and varies repeated-data presentation, which may reduce order-overfitting / improve gradient decorrelation and lower val_bpb. Content unchanged (order only).","fingerprint":"e61a2355a1e89ba2","intervention_predictions":[{"expected_change":"SHUFFLE_DATA=1 lowers 2000-step val_bpb vs sequential baseline","intervention_id":"int_shuffle_data"}],"mechanism_id":"mech_data_order_constrained","name":"Data-order policy in the constrained repeated-data regime","notes":"Data-order axis.","observable_predictions":[{"expected_pattern":"decorrelated/lower at matched step","observable_id":"obs_raw_train_loss"}],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"literature","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel","regime":"constrained repeated-data ~1.6 epoch","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"active","tags":["data_order","curriculum","explore"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["Demon is active in the baseline (it is, endpoint 0.55).","Tracking current gradients more closely late helps rather than adds noise."],"causal_chain":["Late training: baseline beta1 -> 0.55.","Lower endpoint 0.40 -> optimizer weights current gradients more.","Sharper late descent in the warmdown.","Lower final val_bpb."],"claim_ids":["clm_demon_1","clm_demon_2"],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"Demon decays the Adam/Demon beta1 toward the end of training so the optimizer tracks current gradients more closely during the warmdown. The baseline decays to 0.55; a lower endpoint (0.40) tracks even more closely late in training, which may sharpen the final descent and lower val_bpb -- an OPTIMIZER-axis lever distinct from the n-gram/data axes.","fingerprint":"aeb7c946eeeb0993","intervention_predictions":[{"expected_change":"DEMON_FINAL_BETA1=0.40 lowers 2000-step val_bpb vs 0.55","intervention_id":"int_demon_beta1_040"}],"mechanism_id":"mech_demon_momentum_decay","name":"Demon momentum-decay endpoint (optimizer axis)","notes":"Optimizer-axis diversification.","observable_predictions":[{"expected_pattern":"sharper late-training descent","observable_id":"obs_raw_train_loss"}],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"literature","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel","regime":"underfit 2000-step","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"active","tags":["optimizer","demon","explore"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["Packed rows contain resolvable document/BOS boundaries.","Cross-doc attention is net-harmful in this regime.","seg mask does not starve short documents of context."],"causal_chain":["Baseline packs multiple docs per row and attends causally across their boundaries.","Cross-document attention injects spurious context from unrelated prior docs.","Segment mask (seg) makes attention block-diagonal per document (SDPA path only).","Cleaner in-document context -> lower per-token loss -> lower val_bpb."],"claim_ids":[],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"Packed training sequences concatenate multiple documents; the frozen baseline attends causally ACROSS document boundaries. Motivated by FineWeb/modded-nanoGPT: a block-diagonal segment mask (DOC_MASK_MODE=seg) blocks cross-document attention, removing spurious cross-doc signal without restricting the within-document receptive field. Orthogonal to n-gram value-embedding capacity; changes attention connectivity only.","fingerprint":"65fda5be208fb9ad","intervention_predictions":[],"mechanism_id":"mech_doc_boundary_attention","name":"Intra-document attention masking","notes":"New subsystem (attention connectivity). SDPA-only lever, already in frozen train.py.","observable_predictions":[],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"self_proposed","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel","regime":"constrained repeated-data ~1.6 epoch","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"proposed","tags":["attention","doc_mask","structure","explore"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["BOS boundaries resolvable.","Positional packing artifact is net-harmful.","Reset compounds with (not redundant to) seg masking."],"causal_chain":["Baseline uses monotonic RoPE positions across packed doc boundaries.","A token's absolute position depends on preceding docs' lengths (packing artifact).","RESET_ROPE restarts position at each BOS -> canonical intra-doc positions.","Combined with seg attention mask, positions and connectivity are both intra-document -> lower val_bpb."],"claim_ids":[],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"With packed multi-document rows, the baseline assigns monotonic RoPE positions across document boundaries, so token 5 of doc B carries an absolute position reflecting doc A's length. RESET_ROPE restarts rotary position indices at each BOS, making positional encoding intra-document -- the positional partner of the seg attention mask. Expected to compound with seg: attention already blocks cross-doc, and reset removes the misleading cross-doc positional offset, so relative positions within a document become canonical regardless of packing.","fingerprint":"487f6ab329f45101","intervention_predictions":[],"mechanism_id":"mech_intra_document_position","name":"Intra-document RoPE position reset","notes":"Positional-encoding axis; designed partner of DOC_MASK seg.","observable_predictions":[],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"self_proposed","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel","regime":"constrained repeated-data ~1.6 epoch","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"proposed","tags":["position","rope","doc_mask","structure","explore"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["SwiGLU params trainable in 2000 steps.","Gating helps more than the extra params cost.","Interacts benignly with Muon/QK-norm/VE stack."],"causal_chain":["Baseline MLP = squared-ReLU (pointwise nonlinearity).","SwiGLU adds a multiplicative gate (SiLU(a)*b) -> richer feature interactions.","If the extra expressivity is trainable within 2000 steps, per-token loss drops.","Lower loss -> lower val_bpb."],"claim_ids":[],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"The baseline MLP uses squared-ReLU (sqrelu): up-proj -> ReLU^2 -> down-proj. SwiGLU replaces it with a gated linear unit: two projections, one gated by SiLU, elementwise-multiplied, then down-proj. SwiGLU is the dominant MLP in modern LLMs (PaLM/LLaMA); it can model multiplicative feature interactions sqrelu cannot, sometimes improving loss at matched params. Whether it helps in this small repeated-data regime is the open question -- gating adds a projection and may be under-trained at 2000 steps.","fingerprint":"5b5b84511443e590","intervention_predictions":[],"mechanism_id":"mech_mlp_gated_activation","name":"Gated MLP activation (SwiGLU vs ReLU^2)","notes":"MLP-architecture axis; test on the 256x+seg SOTA.","observable_predictions":[],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"self_proposed","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel","regime":"constrained repeated-data ~1.6 epoch","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"proposed","tags":["mlp","architecture","swiglu","explore"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["Lever trainable at 2000 steps.","Effect orthogonal to capacity+seg."],"causal_chain":["Baseline adds residual branches unscaled.","RESID_SCALE scales residual contributions.","Better-conditioned residual stream -> improved optimization.","Lower loss -> lower val_bpb."],"claim_ids":[],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"Residual scaling multiplies residual-branch contributions by a (learned or fixed) factor, controlling signal growth across depth; can improve optimization conditioning.","fingerprint":"5ae92d1ff56aa161","intervention_predictions":[],"mechanism_id":"mech_residscale","name":"residual scaling","notes":"residual scaling on 256x+seg SOTA.","observable_predictions":[],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"self_proposed","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel","regime":"constrained repeated-data ~1.6 epoch","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"proposed","tags":["residscale","explore"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["Lever trainable at 2000 steps.","Effect orthogonal to capacity+seg."],"causal_chain":["Adam group updates can move against the instantaneous gradient (momentum overshoot).","Cautious masking zeros the misaligned components.","More conservative, better-aligned steps -> improved convergence.","Lower loss -> lower val_bpb."],"claim_ids":[],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"Cautious optimization (Liang et al. 2024) masks optimizer updates where the update sign disagrees with the current gradient sign, applying only aligned components. CAUTIOUS_MUON is already on; this extends cautious masking to the Adam group (embeddings/scalars).","fingerprint":"47883ac0cf372e80","intervention_predictions":[],"mechanism_id":"mech_cautupd","name":"cautious Adam update","notes":"cautious Adam update on 256x+seg SOTA.","observable_predictions":[],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"self_proposed","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel","regime":"constrained repeated-data ~1.6 epoch","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"proposed","tags":["cautupd","explore"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["Packed rows contain resolvable document/BOS boundaries.","Cross-doc attention is net-harmful in this regime.","seg mask does not starve short documents of context."],"causal_chain":["seg mask already blocks cross-document attention.","'both' adds a sliding window restricting attention to the last W tokens.","Local focus may help recent-context prediction but drops long in-doc context.","Net val_bpb change unknown -> test."],"claim_ids":[],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"DOC_MASK_MODE=both combines the seg mask (no cross-document attention) with a sliding window (each token attends only to the last W positions). Local windowing can sharpen recent-context modeling and is used in speedrun configs, but it also DISCARDS long-range in-document context. Net effect in this regime is the open question.","fingerprint":"60bb1a2cfb54bc76","intervention_predictions":[],"mechanism_id":"mech_window_plus_seg_attention","name":"Sliding-window + intra-document attention","notes":"Attention-structure probe on the SOTA.","observable_predictions":[],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"self_proposed","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel","regime":"constrained repeated-data ~1.6 epoch","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"proposed","tags":["attention","window","doc_mask","explore"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["VE LR is not already optimal at 256x.","Halving is in the useful range."],"causal_chain":["256x VE tables are 4x larger than the tuned-for 64x.","Rare buckets receive sparse, noisy updates.","A lower VE LR reduces overshoot on those buckets.","Better-conditioned VE features -> lower val_bpb."],"claim_ids":[],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"The n-gram value-embedding tables (256x, the dominant new param group) are optimized by a dedicated RMSProp group at NGRAM_VE_LR_SCALE x base LR. The scale was tuned at 64x tables; at 256x (4x params, sparser per-bucket updates on rare n-grams) the optimal LR may be lower to reduce noise/overshoot on infrequently-hit buckets.","fingerprint":"245d69b5c01c90f9","intervention_predictions":[{"expected_change":"vs this control, an enriched hashed n-gram value-embedding (larger tables / added gated trigram path / retuned sparse LR) lowers 2000-step val_bpb beyond the 0.0007 floor","intervention_id":"int_current_schedule"}],"mechanism_id":"mech_ngram_ve_lr_scaling","name":"N-gram VE optimizer LR scaling","notes":"Tuning the winning capacity structure's optimizer.","observable_predictions":[{"expected_pattern":"lower at matched step vs baseline when n-gram memory is enriched","observable_id":"obs_raw_train_loss"},{"expected_pattern":"watch for widening (memorization) if n-gram capacity is raised too far in the repeated-data regime","observable_id":"obs_train_val_gap"}],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"self_proposed","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT (rsi/daniel)","regime":"repeated-data (~1.6 epoch)","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"active","tags":["ngram","ve","lr","explore"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["Schedule gain persists on top of 256x+seg.","0.75 is still near-optimal at the SOTA."],"causal_chain":["Default WARMDOWN_RATIO=0.95.","0.75 adjusts the LR warmdown timing (proven -0.0007 pre-capacity).","Schedule ~ orthogonal to capacity/structure.","May stack additively on the SOTA -> slightly lower val_bpb."],"claim_ids":[],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"WARMDOWN_RATIO controls when/how the LR warms down over training. 0.75 (vs default 0.95) gave -0.0007 vs baseline pre-capacity. Schedule effects are often orthogonal to model-capacity/structure changes, so the small warmdown gain may stack additively on the 256x+seg SOTA.","fingerprint":"851bffad2476298c","intervention_predictions":[{"expected_change":"small negative delta; near/below seed floor","intervention_id":"int_warmdown_075"}],"mechanism_id":"mech_warmdown_schedule_stack","name":"LR warmdown-ratio on the SOTA","notes":"Additive schedule stack on the SOTA.","observable_predictions":[{"expected_pattern":"lower final val_loss at longer stable phase","observable_id":"obs_val_loss"}],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"self_proposed","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel","regime":"underfit","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"active","tags":["schedule","warmdown","explore"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["The regime overfits enough for WD to matter.","0.15 is in the useful range (not over-regularizing)."],"causal_chain":["Repeated-data (~1.6 epoch) allows mild train overfitting.","Higher weight decay constrains weights.","Better generalization to the held-out val shard.","Lower val_bpb."],"claim_ids":[],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"With ~1.6 epochs over 10 fixed shards, the model can partially memorize training tokens, inflating held-out val_bpb. Higher weight decay (0.1->0.15) constrains weight norms, trading a little train fit for better generalization. Orthogonal to capacity/attention (a regularization knob), potentially additive.","fingerprint":"3f944487dba69dad","intervention_predictions":[{"expected_change":"SHUFFLE_DATA=1 lowers 2000-step val_bpb vs sequential baseline","intervention_id":"int_shuffle_data"}],"mechanism_id":"mech_weight_decay_regularization","name":"Weight decay regularization in repeated-data regime","notes":"Regularization lead for the repeated-data regime.","observable_predictions":[{"expected_pattern":"decorrelated/lower at matched step","observable_id":"obs_raw_train_loss"}],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"self_proposed","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel","regime":"constrained repeated-data ~1.6 epoch","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"active","tags":["regularization","weight_decay","explore"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["Full decay helps at 2000 steps.","Interacts benignly with warmdown."],"causal_chain":["Default final LR = 5% of peak.","0.0 decays fully to zero by step 2000.","Lower end LR -> less late-training noise, sharper minimum.","Lower val_bpb."],"claim_ids":[],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"FINAL_LR_FRAC sets the final LR as a fraction of peak. Default 0.05 leaves a 5% floor at the end; 0.0 decays fully to zero (common in speedruns). A lower final LR can sharpen convergence in the last steps. Schedule-family, potentially additive like warmdown.","fingerprint":"1c5c242276e06ab1","intervention_predictions":[{"expected_change":"small negative delta; near/below seed floor","intervention_id":"int_warmdown_075"}],"mechanism_id":"mech_final_lr_decay","name":"Full LR decay-to-zero","notes":"Schedule-family probe.","observable_predictions":[{"expected_pattern":"lower final val_loss at longer stable phase","observable_id":"obs_val_loss"}],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"self_proposed","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel","regime":"underfit","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"active","tags":["schedule","final_lr","explore"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["Tying trainable with the tuned separate-LR setup."],"causal_chain":["Baseline: separate input embed + output unembed matrices, different LRs.","TIE_EMBED shares one matrix.","Fewer params / coupled in-out representation.","Net val_bpb effect unknown -> test."],"claim_ids":[],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"TIE_EMBED shares one matrix for input token embedding and output unembedding. Reduces params and can regularize, but the baseline tunes embedding LR (0.6) and unembedding LR (0.004) separately and applies a logit softcap; tying couples these and may hurt.","fingerprint":"0ed52eebeaef5ad3","intervention_predictions":[],"mechanism_id":"mech_tied_embeddings","name":"Tied input/output embeddings","notes":"Last embedding-structure probe.","observable_predictions":[],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"self_proposed","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel","regime":"constrained repeated-data ~1.6 epoch","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"proposed","tags":["embedding","tie","explore"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["Init scale matters at 2000 steps."],"causal_chain":["Default lm_head init std 0.001 (near-zero).","0.0 -> exactly uniform initial logits.","Cleaner early-training gradient signal.","Possibly slightly lower val_bpb."],"claim_ids":[],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"LM_HEAD_INIT_STD sets the std of the output projection init. Default 0.001 is near-zero; 0.0 makes initial logits exactly uniform (max-entropy start), which can give cleaner early gradients. Init-scale lever, likely small effect.","fingerprint":"abcd1c60faa7306e","intervention_predictions":[],"mechanism_id":"mech_lm_head_init","name":"Output-head initialization scale","notes":"Last lever probe.","observable_predictions":[],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"self_proposed","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel","regime":"constrained repeated-data ~1.6 epoch","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"proposed","tags":["init","lm_head","explore"],"version":1}
{"assumptions":["Row-wise second moment is dynamically ~equivalent to per-element for row-atomic tables.","Capacity gains continue above 256x (signal-fraction curve unsaturated)."],"causal_chain":["N-gram table subsystem VRAM = params + dense grads + optimizer state (~3x table bytes).","Row-wise second moment cuts the state term to ~0.","Freed VRAM raises the feasible NGRAM_TABLE_MULT.","Higher mult raises per-lookup signal fraction (less collision interference) -> lower val_bpb."],"claim_ids":[],"competing_mechanism_ids":[],"description":"The n-gram VE subsystem costs ~3x its table bytes: params + dense nn.Embedding gradients + RMSProp exp_avg_sq (all ~1x table bytes each), plus ~25GB cudagraph pools. That overhead -- not table storage -- is what caps NGRAM_TABLE_MULT at 256x (512x OOMs). Table rows are gathered/updated row-atomically, so per-element second moments are near-redundant within a row; collapsing exp_avg_sq to an Adafactor-style (rows,1) fp32 row mean-square cuts state from 1x table-bytes to ~1/192. MEASURED: 256x+seg peak VRAM 91.8GB -> 70.5GB (-21.4GB). The freed VRAM buys higher NGRAM_TABLE_MULT, which is the proven dominant axis (capacity gave -0.0040, and trigram signal-fraction is only 0.354 at 256x -- far from saturated).","fingerprint":"128509d30399b1a3","intervention_predictions":[{"expected_change":"vs this control, an enriched hashed n-gram value-embedding (larger tables / added gated trigram path / retuned sparse LR) lowers 2000-step val_bpb beyond the 0.0007 floor","intervention_id":"int_current_schedule"}],"mechanism_id":"mech_ngram_state_rowwise","name":"Row-wise optimizer state frees VRAM for more n-gram capacity","notes":"Enables the capacity axis beyond the 256x hardware cap.","observable_predictions":[{"expected_pattern":"lower at matched step vs baseline when n-gram memory is enriched","observable_id":"obs_raw_train_loss"},{"expected_pattern":"watch for widening (memorization) if n-gram capacity is raised too far in the repeated-data regime","observable_id":"obs_train_val_gap"}],"observation_ids":[],"origin_type":"self_proposed","scope":{"hardware":"single H200","max_steps":2000,"model":"nanoGPT (rsi/daniel)","regime":"repeated-data (~1.6 epoch)","stop_mode":"steps"},"status":"active","tags":["ngram","capacity","optimizer","vram","explore"],"version":1}
{"mechanism_id": "mech_cudagraph_launch_overhead", "version": 1, "description": "In the 5-min frame (STOP_MODE=time, TIME_BUDGET=300) endpoint val_bpb is set by how many optimizer steps finish in 300s. Under COMPILE_MODE=max-autotune-no-cudagraphs every step re-dispatches each kernel from the CPU, paying per-op launch/dispatch overhead. max-autotune (CUDA-graph capture) records the step's kernel sequence once and replays it, removing most of that overhead -> shorter wall-time per step -> more steps in 300s at IDENTICAL per-step math. The baseline disabled cudagraphs only to free the ~25GB graph pool for larger n-gram tables in the STEP-budgeted frame, where step-time was explicitly stated not to affect val_bpb; that rationale does not transfer to a time-budgeted frame.", "origin_type": "mixed", "claim_ids": ["clm_fa3_intra_kernel_scope"], "observation_ids": [], "causal_chain": ["Time-budgeted frame: fewer wall-clock seconds per step => more optimizer steps in 300s => lower endpoint val_bpb (curve slope still negative).", "max-autotune-no-cudagraphs re-dispatches every kernel from the CPU each step, paying per-op launch/dispatch overhead that lengthens step wall-time.", "max-autotune captures the kernel sequence into a CUDA graph and replays it, removing most per-step CPU launch overhead => shorter step wall-time.", "Same kernels in the same order => per-step math identical => matched-step quality unchanged; the endpoint improves only via additional steps.", "Risk path: cudagraph private pools reserve ~25GB; if that OOMs alongside the frame's n-gram tables the treatment fails outright rather than improving."], "assumptions": ["The ~25GB cudagraph pool fits in H200 VRAM with the frame's baseline tables.", "Per-step CPU launch overhead is a non-trivial fraction of step wall-time at this model size.", "The val_bpb-vs-steps curve near the 300s budget still has negative slope."], "competing_mechanism_ids": [], "scope": {"frame": "walltime_5min_h200", "applies_to": "time_budgeted_training"}, "observable_predictions": [{"observable_id": "obs_step_time_ms", "expected_pattern": "treatment lower than control"}, {"observable_id": "obs_tok_per_sec_k", "expected_pattern": "treatment higher than control"}], "intervention_predictions": [{"intervention_id": "int_fa3_cudagraphs", "expected_change": "lower step_time_ms, more optimizer steps within 300s, lower endpoint val_bpb at identical per-step math (unless it OOMs)"}], "name": "CUDA-graph capture removes per-step launch overhead in a time-budgeted frame", "status": "proposed", "tags": ["systems_kernel", "cudagraph", "compile", "throughput", "walltime_5min"], "notes": "", "fingerprint": "b220e89be53994cf"}
{"mechanism_id": "mech_ngram_sparse_grad_memory_traffic", "version": 1, "description": "The frozen baseline builds n-gram VE tables at 64x and, each step, autograd materializes a dense (~5.6 GB at 64x) bf16 gradient over them and the optimizer keeps a full (rows,dim) second moment. NGRAM_SPARSE_GRAD routes the gather through a custom op whose backward writes only the touched (indices, grad_rows) -- ~1.6 GB, constant in table size -- and NGRAM_STATE_ROWWISE collapses the second moment to (rows,1). Less DRAM traffic and fewer bytes moved per step => shorter step wall-time => more optimizer steps in the 300s budget, at match-to-rounding numerics.", "origin_type": "mixed", "claim_ids": ["clm_galore_memory_lever"], "observation_ids": [], "causal_chain": ["Time-budgeted frame: shorter step wall-time => more steps in 300s => lower endpoint val_bpb.", "Baseline materializes+reduces a dense ~5.6 GB n-gram gradient and a full (rows,dim) optimizer second moment every step -- heavy DRAM traffic.", "NGRAM_SPARSE_GRAD writes only touched rows (~1.6 GB, constant) and NGRAM_STATE_ROWWISE shrinks state to (rows,1), cutting bytes moved per step.", "Fewer bytes moved => shorter step wall-time, with numerics matching to rounding (fp32 row sums vs bf16 atomics) => quality-per-step ~unchanged.", "Risk: at 64x the dense gradient may be small enough that the custom-op overhead cancels the saving, or grad_accum!=1 forbids the path -> no gain."], "assumptions": ["The frame runs grad_accum_steps==1 (required by NGRAM_SPARSE_GRAD).", "Dense n-gram gradient + optimizer-state traffic is a non-trivial share of step wall-time at 64x.", "match-to-rounding numerics do not shift matched-step val_bpb materially."], "competing_mechanism_ids": [], "scope": {"frame": "walltime_5min_h200", "applies_to": "time_budgeted_training_with_ngram_tables"}, "observable_predictions": [{"observable_id": "obs_step_time_ms", "expected_pattern": "treatment lower than control"}, {"observable_id": "obs_mfu_percent", "expected_pattern": "treatment >= control"}], "intervention_predictions": [{"intervention_id": "int_ngram_sparse_grad", "expected_change": "lower step_time_ms, more steps in 300s, lower endpoint val_bpb at match-to-rounding numerics (unless overhead cancels the saving at 64x)"}], "name": "Sparse-grad + row-wise state cut per-step n-gram memory traffic", "status": "proposed", "tags": ["memory_retrieval", "ngram", "sparse_grad", "throughput", "walltime_5min"], "notes": "", "fingerprint": "db7dcc770654e264"}
{"mechanism_id": "mech_cautious_adam_updates", "version": 1, "description": "The optimizer already applies cautious masking to Muon params (CAUTIOUS_MUON=1) but not to the Adam-updated params (CAUTIOUS_UPDATE=0). Cautious masking zeroes the momentum-update components whose sign disagrees with the current gradient, so the step never moves a coordinate against its instantaneous gradient. Applied to the Adam params (embeddings, scalars, head), this should improve descent quality per step at negligible compute (an elementwise mask), leaving step throughput ~unchanged -> lower endpoint val_bpb in the fixed-time frame.", "origin_type": "mixed", "claim_ids": ["clm_recent_optim_cautious_1"], "observation_ids": [], "causal_chain": ["Cautious mask zeroes update components whose sign disagrees with the gradient => no coordinate moves against its instantaneous gradient.", "Applied to the Adam-side params (currently uncautious), this removes locally-counterproductive steps => better loss descent per optimizer step.", "The mask is an elementwise op => step wall-time ~unchanged => step count in 300s ~unchanged.", "Better quality-per-step at equal steps => lower endpoint val_bpb."], "assumptions": ["The Adam-side params contribute enough of the update that cautioning them matters.", "The benefit is not already saturated by CAUTIOUS_MUON on the Muon params."], "competing_mechanism_ids": [], "scope": {"frame": "walltime_5min_h200", "applies_to": "adam_updated_parameters"}, "observable_predictions": [{"observable_id": "obs_val_loss", "expected_pattern": "treatment reaches lower val loss at matched steps"}], "intervention_predictions": [{"intervention_id": "int_cautious_update", "expected_change": "lower endpoint val_bpb at ~equal steps; step_time_ms ~unchanged"}], "name": "Cautious masking on Adam-side params improves per-step quality", "status": "proposed", "tags": ["optimizer", "cautious", "walltime_5min"], "notes": "", "fingerprint": "f1507b5006a8c005"}
{"mechanism_id": "mech_muon_momentum_continuity", "version": 1, "description": "The Muon momentum warmup is step-anchored (min(step/300,1)) while the warmdown is progress-anchored. At progress>0.05 (~step110) the warmup has only reached ~0.887 and the warmdown branch snaps momentum to 0.95 (+0.063 discontinuity) then decays to 0.79. The intended warmup is dead code and peak momentum is never held. Re-anchoring the warmup to progress removes the discontinuity in the highest-LR window (where updates are most impactful), giving a cleaner momentum trajectory and better per-step descent at zero wall-time cost.", "origin_type": "mixed", "claim_ids": ["clm_demon_1"], "observation_ids": [], "causal_chain": ["Momentum sets update smoothing/effective step size (Demon: momentum scheduling changes final performance).", "The current schedule has a +0.063 momentum discontinuity at ~step110, in the highest-LR window where updates matter most.", "Re-anchoring warmup to progress makes momentum continuous and lets it reach peak exactly at warmdown onset.", "A cleaner momentum trajectory in the high-LR window improves per-step descent -> lower endpoint val_bpb, at zero wall-time (schedule-only change)."], "assumptions": ["The momentum discontinuity in the high-LR window measurably hurts convergence.", "The effect is not washed out by the short (~110 step) affected window."], "competing_mechanism_ids": [], "scope": {"frame": "walltime_5min_h200", "applies_to": "muon_momentum_schedule"}, "observable_predictions": [{"observable_id": "obs_muon_momentum", "expected_pattern": "continuous ramp, no jump at ~step110"}], "intervention_predictions": [{"intervention_id": "int_muon_momentum_continuous", "expected_change": "lower endpoint val_bpb at ~equal steps; momentum trajectory continuous"}], "name": "Continuous Muon momentum schedule in the high-LR window", "status": "proposed", "tags": ["optimizer", "muon", "momentum", "walltime_5min"], "notes": "", "fingerprint": "6d9c25602e10c767"}
{"mechanism_id": "mech_rho1_focal_reweight", "version": 1, "description": "RHO1_GAMMA upweights each token's CE loss by w=ce**gamma (normalized to preserve scale), so each step's gradient concentrates on tokens the model has not yet learned. In the underfit 2000-step regime this raises useful learning-per-step at negligible wall-time (one extra reduction='none' CE), lowering endpoint val_bpb -- unless the self-referential focal upweights unlearnable/noisy tokens.", "origin_type": "mixed", "claim_ids": ["clm_r2_data_rho1_selective_lm_1"], "observation_ids": [], "causal_chain": ["Token-selective/weighted loss improves data efficiency (Rho-1).", "Focal w=ce**gamma upweights high-loss (unlearned) tokens, normalized to preserve loss scale.", "Gradient concentrates on informative tokens -> more useful learning per step in an underfit regime.", "At negligible extra compute -> lower endpoint val_bpb at ~equal steps."], "assumptions": ["High-loss tokens are informative-and-learnable, not noise.", "gamma small enough to not destabilize (cap <=0.5)."], "competing_mechanism_ids": [], "scope": {"frame": "walltime_5min_h200", "applies_to": "training_loss_token_weighting"}, "observable_predictions": [{"observable_id": "obs_val_loss", "expected_pattern": "treatment reaches lower val loss at matched steps"}], "intervention_predictions": [{"intervention_id": "int_rho1_gamma_020", "expected_change": "lower endpoint val_bpb at ~equal steps; risk of null/worse if focal upweights noise"}], "name": "Focal reweighting concentrates gradient on high-loss tokens", "status": "proposed", "tags": ["data_order", "rho1", "focal_loss", "walltime_5min"], "notes": "", "fingerprint": "fa624e93a781e87f"}
{"mechanism_id": "mech_ngram_rowwise_state_traffic", "version": 1, "description": "NGRAM_STATE_ROWWISE collapses the n-gram VE second-moment optimizer state from (rows,dim) to (rows,1) via a compiled fused RMSProp kernel, removing ~full-table second-moment read+write traffic per step. Cheaper steps -> more steps in 300s at match-to-rounding numerics. This is the GOOD compiled half of the confounded sparse-grad experiment, never measured solo in frame B.", "origin_type": "mixed", "claim_ids": ["clm_galore_memory_lever"], "observation_ids": [], "causal_chain": ["Time-budgeted frame: cheaper steps -> more steps in 300s -> lower val_bpb.", "Baseline keeps a full (rows,dim) n-gram second moment, read+written every step.", "Row-wise (rows,1) state via the fused kernel cuts that second-moment traffic.", "Fewer bytes/step -> shorter step -> more steps, match-to-rounding numerics."], "assumptions": ["The second-moment traffic is a non-trivial share of step time at 64x.", "(rows,1) state is dynamically ~equivalent to per-element for row-atomic tables."], "competing_mechanism_ids": ["mech_ngram_sparse_grad_memory_traffic"], "scope": {"frame": "walltime_5min_h200", "applies_to": "ngram_optimizer_state"}, "observable_predictions": [{"observable_id": "obs_step_time_ms", "expected_pattern": "treatment lower"}], "intervention_predictions": [{"intervention_id": "int_ngram_rowwise_state", "expected_change": "lower step_time_ms, more steps"}], "name": "Row-wise n-gram optimizer state cuts per-step memory traffic", "status": "proposed", "tags": ["memory_retrieval", "ngram", "throughput", "walltime_5min"], "notes": "", "fingerprint": "5086e2f969aa6ec7"}
{"mechanism_id": "mech_ns_steps", "version": 1, "description": "Muon orthogonalizes each 2D param via 5 Newton-Schulz iterations. Dropping to 4 cuts per-step orthogonalization compute, giving a faster Muon step and more steps in 300s, at coarser orthogonalization. Small matrices (d=768) make the NS share of step time small, so likely sub-gate.", "origin_type": "mixed", "claim_ids": [], "observation_ids": [], "causal_chain": ["Time-budgeted frame: cheaper/steadier steps yield a better endpoint.", "Muon orthogonalizes each 2D param via 5 Newton-Schulz iterations. Dropping to 4 cuts per-step orthogonalization compute,", "Net effect measured by the stage-1 pilot."], "assumptions": ["The knob's effect is non-negligible at this scale."], "competing_mechanism_ids": [], "scope": {"frame": "walltime_5min_h200"}, "observable_predictions": [{"observable_id": "obs_step_time_ms", "expected_pattern": "see hypothesis"}], "intervention_predictions": [{"intervention_id": "int_ns_steps", "expected_change": "see hypothesis"}], "name": "Fewer Newton-Schulz iterations (MUON_NS_STEPS 5 to 4)", "status": "proposed", "tags": ["optimizer", "ns_steps", "walltime_5min"], "notes": "", "fingerprint": "e0207b2d8315c6e2"}
{"mechanism_id": "mech_adam_beta2", "version": 1, "description": "The Adam-side groups (embeddings, lm_head, VEs) use beta2=0.95, a short second-moment window for high-variance sparsely-hit rows. A longer beta2 of 0.98 stabilizes per-row effective LR for cleaner per-step updates at zero wall-time. Near a tuned value so likely sub-floor.", "origin_type": "mixed", "claim_ids": [], "observation_ids": [], "causal_chain": ["Time-budgeted frame: cheaper/steadier steps yield a better endpoint.", "The Adam-side groups (embeddings, lm_head, VEs) use beta2=0.95, a short second-moment window for high-variance sparsely-", "Net effect measured by the stage-1 pilot."], "assumptions": ["The knob's effect is non-negligible at this scale."], "competing_mechanism_ids": [], "scope": {"frame": "walltime_5min_h200"}, "observable_predictions": [{"observable_id": "obs_step_time_ms", "expected_pattern": "see hypothesis"}], "intervention_predictions": [{"intervention_id": "int_adam_beta2", "expected_change": "see hypothesis"}], "name": "Longer Adam second-moment window (ADAM_BETA2 0.95 to 0.98)", "status": "proposed", "tags": ["optimizer", "adam_beta2", "walltime_5min"], "notes": "", "fingerprint": "41424eb4c01e3260"}
{"mechanism_id": "mech_ve_eps", "version": 1, "description": "The n-gram VE RMSProp groups use eps=1e-10, so a rarely-hit row's first updates get a near-1/sqrt(v) explosion. A larger eps of 1e-8 damps first-touch overshoot for rare n-grams for steadier rare-token learning per step. Step-neutral.", "origin_type": "mixed", "claim_ids": [], "observation_ids": [], "causal_chain": ["Time-budgeted frame: cheaper/steadier steps yield a better endpoint.", "The n-gram VE RMSProp groups use eps=1e-10, so a rarely-hit row's first updates get a near-1/sqrt(v) explosion. A larger", "Net effect measured by the stage-1 pilot."], "assumptions": ["The knob's effect is non-negligible at this scale."], "competing_mechanism_ids": [], "scope": {"frame": "walltime_5min_h200"}, "observable_predictions": [{"observable_id": "obs_step_time_ms", "expected_pattern": "see hypothesis"}], "intervention_predictions": [{"intervention_id": "int_ve_eps", "expected_change": "see hypothesis"}], "name": "Larger n-gram VE RMSProp eps (1e-10 to 1e-8)", "status": "proposed", "tags": ["memory_retrieval", "ve_eps", "walltime_5min"], "notes": "", "fingerprint": "5f771a56301972cf"}
{"mechanism_id": "mech_bf16_ce", "version": 1, "description": "The training cross-entropy currently casts the full [B*T, vocab] logits to float32 before F.cross_entropy, materializing the single largest activation at 2x its bf16 size. Computing CE directly in bf16 skips that float32 materialization, cutting the memory + bandwidth of the largest tensor and buying steps in the 300s budget. Eval val_bpb still uses float32 logits, so the metric is unaffected; only the training gradient uses a coarser bf16 softmax (logits are softcapped to +/-16.5, so no overflow risk).", "origin_type": "mixed", "claim_ids": [], "observation_ids": [], "causal_chain": ["Time-budgeted frame: cheaper CE step -> more steps -> better endpoint.", "The training cross-entropy currently casts the full [B*T, vocab] logits to float32 before F.cross_entropy, materializing", "Net effect measured by the stage-1 pilot."], "assumptions": ["bf16 softmax over the softcapped logits preserves per-step quality within noise."], "competing_mechanism_ids": [], "scope": {"frame": "walltime_5min_h200"}, "observable_predictions": [{"observable_id": "obs_step_time_ms", "expected_pattern": "decrease"}], "intervention_predictions": [{"intervention_id": "int_bf16_ce", "expected_change": "more steps in 300s"}], "name": "bf16 cross-entropy (skip float32 logits materialization)", "status": "proposed", "tags": ["systems_kernel", "bf16_ce", "walltime_5min", "throughput"], "notes": "", "fingerprint": "21d98eaa1e5556a9"}
{"mechanism_id": "mech_canon", "version": 1, "description": "Insert a cheap causal depthwise 1-D convolution (kernel 4) into each block's residual stream before attention: x += sum_k w[:,k]*x[t-k] on norm(x). Explicit learned short-range token mixing (Canon layers, Allen-Zhu 2025) that a pure attention+VE stack acquires only slowly; underfit ~1976-step regime -> a cheap learned local prior is a quality-per-step gain. Fused shifted-weighted-sum (no transpose): +7.8% step-time measured, vs +34% naive. Zero-init -> byte-identical off.", "origin_type": "mixed", "claim_ids": [], "observation_ids": [], "causal_chain": ["Underfit: a cheap learned local-mixing prior speeds convergence per step.", "Insert a cheap causal depthwise 1-D convolution (kernel 4) into each block's residual stream before attention: x += sum_", "Net effect measured by the stage-1 pilot."], "assumptions": ["Short-range channel mixing is a convergence bottleneck the stack learns slowly."], "competing_mechanism_ids": [], "scope": {"frame": "walltime_5min_h200"}, "observable_predictions": [{"observable_id": "obs_step_time_ms", "expected_pattern": "small increase"}], "intervention_predictions": [{"intervention_id": "int_canon", "expected_change": "lower val_bpb per step"}], "name": "Canon causal depthwise conv (refined fused shifted-sum)", "status": "proposed", "tags": ["architecture", "canon", "walltime_5min", "explore"], "notes": "", "fingerprint": "aa5d2a833549aa6f"}
